# 📊 COVID-19 Interactive Executive Dashboard (Tableau)

## 📌 Project Overview
This project focuses on translating complex data insights into highly scannable, executive-ready data visualizations. Taking the cleansed and aggregated global datasets generated from the SQL exploration phase, I designed and deployed a live, interactive data presentation system using **Tableau Public**.

The resulting dashboard serves as a functional Business Intelligence tool, enabling stakeholders to interactively filter global parameters, map historical geographic spreads, and track runtime infection speeds across multiple global regions simultaneously.

---

## 🛠️ Visual & Technical Features Demonstrated
*   **Interactive Controls:** Global Year/Month filters, automated country multi-selection parameters.
*   **Geographic Choropleth Maps:** Color-density mapping synced with continuous data scales to illustrate macro-level virus distribution.
*   **Time-Series Continuity:** Transforming discrete date blocks into fluid continuous timelines to monitor regional wave peaks.
*   **Executive KPI Cards:** Consolidated summary blocks indicating worldwide metrics instantly.

---

## 🚀 Dynamic Visual Fixes & Data Normalization

### 1. Rectifying Aggregation Outliers (`SUM` vs. `MAX` Logic)
*   **The Visual Bug:** Early configurations of the time-series charts plotted catastrophic infection rates spiking up to 400-500% per country. This occurred because Tableau defaults to auto-aggregating daily metrics using `SUM()`.
*   **The Technical Fix:** Reconfigured the structural shelf measures to calculate using a strict **`MAX()`** aggregation. Since the data tracks the cumulative progress of infections, capturing the maximum recorded calculation per day instantly normalized the metric axes back down to realistic thresholds (0-15%).

### 2. Time-Series Continuity Optimization
*   **The Visualization Challenge:** Standard date groupings segmented charts into disjointed blocks, breaking the natural visual flow of wave trajectories.
*   **The Fix:** Altered the date dimension pills from discrete parts into a **Continuous Month Timeline**, allowing multi-year charts (spanning March 2020 through May 2021) to project a smooth, flowing linear regression curve.

